The Architectural Models:
Central banks use two primary frameworks to handle tokenized reserve settlements.
[1] The Unified / Single Ledger Model: Commercial bank digital money (tokenized deposits) and central bank digital money (tokenized reserves) sit on the exact same ledger platform. 
This configuration is favored in projects like the Regulated Liability Network (RLN) and BIS Project Agorá. 
It allows for flawless synchronization because everything changes ownership in a single ledger update.
[2] The Interoperable / Multi-Ledger Model: Tokenized deposits move on a private commercial banking network (e.g., Canton Network), while tokenized reserves remain on a dedicated, highly secure central bank ledger. 
The two chains communicate via interoperability protocols (like Hash Time-Lock Contracts) to ensure that the commercial token cannot move unless the central bank reserve token moves concurrently.
